# What goes into the source distribution (`python -m build --sdist`).
#
# Setuptools' sdist is additive, not a tree walk: by default it ships only
# the files it already knows about -- pyproject.toml, setup.py, README.md,
# the Python packages, and the .cpp files named as extension sources. It
# ships no headers and no data files at all. Without the rules below the
# sdist is unbuildable: setup.py reads binding/module_config.ini before it
# can even declare the extension, and the .cpp sources include headers from
# include/, src/, and binding/ plus the generated table
# src/core/preflop_rank_table.inc.
#
# Two audiences to serve:
#   1. pip, on a platform with no published wheel -- it downloads the sdist
#      and compiles the extension from it. Section 1 below is what that
#      needs; it is load-bearing, not optional.
#   2. Anyone auditing or rebuilding the project from the sdist alone -- the
#      standalone CMake build, both test suites with their fixtures,
#      examples, benchmarks, and docs. Sections 2-4.
#
# Because the default file set is additive, build output (build/, dist/,
# *.egg-info) and tool caches (.pytest_cache/, __pycache__/) are never
# reached by any rule here; the trailing global-excludes are belt-and-braces
# for the grafted directories.

# --- 1. Build inputs: required for `pip install` from this sdist ---------
# Read by setup.py at import time to resolve the extension name and sources.
include binding/module_config.ini
recursive-include binding *.cpp *.h
recursive-include include *.h
# *.inc picks up src/core/preflop_rank_table.inc, the precomputed preflop
# rank table that eval.cpp #includes directly.
recursive-include src *.cpp *.h *.inc

# --- 2. Python package and its test suite -------------------------------
recursive-include python/xiapl *.py *.pyi py.typed
recursive-include python/test *.py

# --- 3. Standalone C++ build (CMake), examples, benchmarks --------------
include CMakeLists.txt
graft cmake
recursive-include apps *.cpp
recursive-include examples *.cpp
recursive-include benchmarks *.cpp

# --- 4. C++ tests and docs ----------------------------------------------
# tests/ mixes .cpp, a C ABI test in plain .c, bundled doctest.h, and two
# shell guards (check_binding_includes.sh / check_exports.sh) that CMake
# registers as ctests.
recursive-include tests *.cpp *.c *.h *.sh
# Grafted rather than extension-scoped: the fixtures are .phh, .phhs, their
# .expected.json goldens, and a README, and new formats should not need an
# edit here.
graft tests/fixtures
graft docs

# LICENSE is not listed: setuptools' default `license-files` glob
# (LICEN[CS]E*, COPYING*, NOTICE*, AUTHORS*) already sweeps it into both the
# sdist and the wheel's .dist-info. Verified by adding a LICENSE file to a
# scratch copy of this tree and confirming it appeared in both.

# --- 5. On the absence of exclude rules ---------------------------------
# There are none, deliberately, and this file should stay that way.
#
# Because the default file set is additive and every rule above is scoped
# to a named directory, build output (build/, dist/, *.egg-info/) and tool
# caches (.pytest_cache/, __pycache__/) are simply never reachable. A prune
# for them would do nothing except emit "no previously-included directories
# found matching ..." on every single build. Same for .github/ and
# .gitignore, which are repository plumbing the sdist has no use for: not
# named above, therefore not shipped.
#
# The one rule with a real (if small) target was `global-exclude .DS_Store`,
# since the three grafts above take whatever is in those directories and
# macOS litters .DS_Store into any folder opened in Finder. It was dropped
# too: the published sdist is always built by .github/workflows/release.yml
# from a fresh actions/checkout, which cannot contain a .DS_Store, and
# Trusted Publishing means no one can upload a locally built artifact
# instead. The guard would have cost four warning lines per build to
# protect a case that cannot occur.
#
# Net effect: `python -m build` emits zero MANIFEST warnings, so any
# warning that does appear here in future is real signal.
